---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const bioinfCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · The data explosion', note: 'Sequencing generates terabytes daily. A single human genome is ~100 GB processed. Biology became a data science by necessity — bioinformatics is the response.' },
    { math: '2 · Alignment: finding similarity', note: 'Line up sequences to find conserved regions. BLAST (1990) searches your sequence against everything known in seconds — the most-cited paper in biology.' },
    { math: '3 · Assembly and annotation', note: 'Short reads must be stitched into genomes (a jigsaw with 3 billion pieces), then genes, regulatory regions and repeats labelled. Machine learning does much of the labelling now.' },
    { math: '4 · AlphaFold: structure solved', note: 'Predicting a protein\'s 3-D shape from its sequence was biology\'s 50-year grand challenge — AlphaFold (2020) effectively solved it (Nobel 2024, Hassabis & Jumper). 200+ million structures predicted, free for all.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/biology/expert/bioinformatics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Bioinformatics is <strong>biology's data discipline</strong>: algorithms, statistics and
    databases applied to sequences, structures and molecular measurements. Born of necessity
    (genomes outgrew notebooks), it now leads — AI systems predict structures, scan diagnoses
    from tumour genomes, and trace pandemics variant by variant in real time.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the pipeline from raw sequence to biological insight" widget="equationStep" config={bioinfCfg} />

  <h2>Where it delivers</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Genomic medicine:</strong> tumour sequencing tailors cancer treatment; rare-disease diagnoses that took decades of specialist visits now take weeks of pipeline.</li>
    <li><strong>Epidemiology:</strong> viral genome surveillance traced SARS-CoV-2 variants worldwide within days of each emergence — public health steered by phylogenetics.</li>
    <li><strong>Comparative genomics:</strong> reading genomes across species reveals what makes humans human (gene regulation changes more than genes) and what makes pathogens dangerous (which mutations change transmission).</li>
    <li><strong>The skills stack:</strong> command-line fluency, Python/R, algorithms (alignment, graphs), statistics, and enough biology to know which questions matter. The interdisciplinary profile this atlas's CS + biology ladders are designed to feed.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Altschul et al., J. Mol. Biol. (1990); Jumper et al., Nature (2021); Human Genome Project (Nature, 2001/2003).</p>
</TopicPage>
